WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.070
I loaded a Beretta nine millimeter.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:07.393
I put it to my temple and
I pulled the trigger and

00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:09.394
the greatest peace that I
felt was when that hammer

00:00:09.394 --> 00:00:12.253
fell because I knew it was gonna be over.

00:00:13.944 --> 00:00:17.645
It was only a microsecond
but when you spend day in and

00:00:17.645 --> 00:00:22.203
day out suffering, a microsecond
of peace is an eternity.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.344
Since I'm sitting here you obviously

00:00:29.344 --> 00:00:31.660
know that the gun didn't go off.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:32.923
That happened twice.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.280
Different ammunition.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.970
I tried to overdose once and twice

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:38.270
I tried to slit my wrists.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.140
In America, around 22

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.743
military veterans kill
themselves every day.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.650
John served in Iraq during
the second Gulf War.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.300
His experiences left him with

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.790
Post Traumatic Stress Disorder, or PTSD.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.797
The mental suffering has been so great

00:00:56.797 --> 00:00:59.443
that he was suicidal for eight years.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.900
One of the things we never
realized was that a lot

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.290
of us would be asked to
give our life for our

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.900
country, not in Iraq, not
in Afghanistan but far,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.053
far from the battlefield
at home, alone in the dark.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.000
Combat takes an emotional toll.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.430
Not only the things you see,
not only the things you do.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.880
But the base I was on got mortared

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.603
thousands of times in a year.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.126
There's guys on my base
that never went outside

00:01:26.126 --> 00:01:29.291
the wire, that are suffering
from extreme PTSD just

00:01:29.291 --> 00:01:32.343
from the constant fear of raining bombs.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.770
An estimated eight million

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.824
Americans suffer with
PTSD and war veterans

00:01:38.824 --> 00:01:40.833
are only a fraction of this number.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.498
It's a public health disaster costing

00:01:44.498 --> 00:01:46.483
billions of dollars to treat.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.490
There are treatments
available but the drugs

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.993
prescribed are only
successful in 20% of cases.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.593
For those who don't
respond to the available

00:01:56.593 --> 00:02:01.593
treatment, there may be
an alternative, MDMA,

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.850
the active ingredient in
the party drug ecstasy

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.723
is being touted as a
miracle cure for PTSD.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:12.610
The FDA has labeled MDMA
a breakthrough treatment

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.000
and tests of the drug show
that it's highly effective.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.870
In a recent study, over 60%
of patients no longer met

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:23.723
the clinical criteria for
PTSD after the MDMA treatment.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.583
For John, conventional
treatments hadn't worked.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.993
He saw the trial as his last hope.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.200
I'd never taken any psychedelics,
I'd never taken MDMA.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.850
I kind of thought it was
gonna be like The Matrix

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.600
where instantly things
were gonna get magical.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.550
Is it like gradual rise and gradual fall?

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.190
Hopefully, it's waves where
you start to notice something.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:54.200
Sometimes people report a lot of thoughts.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.970
And then it kicked in
after about 30, 40 minutes.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:57.803
No anxiety.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:03.813
Geometric patterns.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.370
And I remember thinking
to myself, I completely

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.180
get why people take this now.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.370
For John, the treatment was a revelation.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.340
It was probably the greatest
therapeutic experience

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.233
I have ever had in my entire life.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.470
What are you experiencing?

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:21.840
It disconnects the
amygdala, that fear response

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.397
in the brain, that fight
or flight and you can

00:03:24.397 --> 00:03:27.113
talk about the trauma without
having a panic attack.

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:29.373
Thinking a lot about.

00:03:32.263 --> 00:03:33.096
In Iraq?

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.490
He lost his leg.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.633
Part of him getting hurt was my fault.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.953
The MDMA puts the brain in a
place where the therapy can

00:03:43.953 --> 00:03:47.893
work and people always ask
describe it, describe it.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.320
The best description I
have come up with is,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.542
it's like doing therapy while
being hugged by everyone who

00:03:54.542 --> 00:03:58.023
loves you in a bathtub full
of puppies licking your face.

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:03.170
MDMA was used in psychotherapy

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.890
in the 1970s but became
popular as a party drug

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.143
in the 1980s and was made illegal.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.010
It wasn't until the early
2000s that the drug began

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.113
scientific testing in
America to treat PTSD.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:21.130
Testing is now at the
final stage of FDA approval

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:24.440
where around 300 subjects
will participate.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:25.920
If this is successful,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.820
then the drug could become a medicine.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.570
Rick Doblin has been at the forefront

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.503
of this research since the 1980s.

00:04:33.503 --> 00:04:35.290
And one of the veterans that was

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.724
in our study was John Lubecky.

00:04:37.724 --> 00:04:39.790
He served 12 years.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.693
We give MDMA only three
times, one month apart,

00:04:42.693 --> 00:04:46.826
with 12 90 minute non-drug
psychotherapy sessions

00:04:46.826 --> 00:04:50.403
and each of the sessions last eight hours.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.059
The current treatments for
PTSD flattened out people's

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:58.467
emotions and they are requiring
people to take these drugs

00:04:58.467 --> 00:05:02.853
every day for months or years
or a lifetime basically.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.770
An estimated 20% of former military

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.283
personnel suffer with
PTSD in America alone.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.360
The Department of Veteran's Affairs

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.229
spends about $400 million
annually on treating

00:05:16.229 --> 00:05:19.453
PTSD and other mental health conditions.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.360
When you take MDMA, it floods the brain

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.030
with hormones and
neurotransmitters that evoke

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:27.853
feelings of trust and wellbeing.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:31.010
Researchers say this allows patients

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.193
to reexamine traumatic memories.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:38.100
Of all the bad shit in Iraq,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.253
the worst thing was coming home.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.264
Like any drug, MDMA has side

00:05:44.264 --> 00:05:47.157
effects and there's a
moderate risk of addiction

00:05:47.157 --> 00:05:50.513
but it is less harmful
than alcohol or tobacco.

00:05:51.703 --> 00:05:54.870
A small nonprofit is funding
the trial as MDMA is not

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.870
patentable so big pharmaceutical
companies have steered

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:00.723
clear of the treatment as
it will not make them money.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.290
For sufferers of PTSD, this new

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:05.993
treatment could be a lifesaver.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:08.793
If hadn't have gone through this,

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:12.823
I'd be in Arlington cemetery.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:20.700
I have a stepson and if it
weren't for this treatment,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.550
he probably would have been
handed the flag off my casket

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:28.063
and somebody would have
had to explain to him why.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.093
Especially knowing that
treatments are available.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.700
They made it so that my stepson
will get the flag off my

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.576
casket when he's old and gray
and I'm old and gray and after

00:06:40.576 --> 00:06:44.440
I've watched him fall in love
and graduate high school,

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:49.440
college, get married, bounce
a grandchild on my knee.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.960
Those are the things that
this treatment provides for

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.493
veterans and everyone rather
than a life cut short.

